module Debug exposing (log, todo)

{-| This module can be useful while _developing_ an application. It is not
available for use in packages or production.

`toString` used to live here and is gone: `inspect` replaced it (D16), and
unlike this module `inspect` is total, pure, pinned (`docs/representation.md`
R5) and available in packages and in optimized builds. It is a default import,
so there is nothing to write to reach it.

@docs log, todo

-}

import Gren.Kernel.Debug
import String exposing (String)


{-| Log a tagged value on the developer console, and then return the value.

    1 + log "number" 1 -- equals 2, logs "number: 1"

    length (log "start" []) -- equals 0, logs "start: []"

It is often possible to sprinkle this around to see if values are what you
expect. It is kind of old-school to do it this way, but it works!

**Note:** This is not available with `gren make --optimize`, for the reason
`syntax.md` S8 gives: it is an impure function typed as a pure one, so the
optimizer would be free to hoist it out of a loop, run it once instead of a
thousand times, or delete it — which is to say it would stop working in exactly
the situations you reach for it. It is a barrier no Core pass may move, and
that is affordable only because it cannot appear in a package or a release
build.

-}
log : String -> a -> a
log =
    Gren.Kernel.Debug.log


{-| This is a placeholder for code that you will write later.

For example, if you are working with a large union type and have partially
completed a case expression, it may make sense to do this:

    type Entity = Ship | Fish | Captain | Seagull

    drawEntity entity =
      case entity of
        Ship ->
          ...

        Fish ->
          ...

        _ ->
          Debug.todo "handle Captain and Seagull"

The Gren compiler recognizes each `Debug.todo` so if you run into it, you get
an **uncatchable runtime exception** that includes the module name and line
number.

**Note:** This is not available with `gren make --optimize` or packages. The
idea is that a `todo` can be useful during development, but uncatchable runtime
exceptions should not appear in the resulting applications.

**Note:** For the equivalent of try/catch error handling in Gren, use modules
like [`Maybe`](#Maybe) and [`Result`](#Result) which guarantee that no error
goes unhandled!

-}
todo : String -> a
todo =
    Gren.Kernel.Debug.todo
